stress - system stress suite for RhapsodiOS

Usage
    run [-a] [-L] [-s seed] [-t secs] [-d dir] [-D dev] cycles [cat ...]

    cat is any of vm mmap fs proc net ipc thread misc (the default) or disk.
    Each cycle runs every test of a category alone, then all of them at once
    (disk tests run one at a time).  Output goes to
    Report/<cat>.out, the totals and every FAIL line to Report/summary, core
    files to Report/cores.  Exit status is 1 if anything failed.

    -a        run the categories at the same time
    -L        fail a test whose kernel resources leak (see Leak report)
    -s seed   random seed (a failure prints it; reuse it to replay)
    -t secs   per-test time limit (default 600)
    -d dir    scratch directory (default /tmp); files named dir/stress.* are
              removed when run starts
    -D dev    raw scratch device for the disk category, e.g. /dev/rhd1a.  The
              tests OVERWRITE its first bytes.  disk is never a default: it
              runs only when named, and naming it without -D exits 2.
              stress-disk refuses a mounted, root or symlinked device.
    cycles    how many times to repeat

    The fs fill test fills a whole file system; it runs only when named and
    only if STRESS_FILL_DIR names a scratch one.  The vm settime test steps the system clock as root,
    so it is opt-in: it runs only as stress-vm settime, and make check-vm runs
    it only if STRESS_SETTIME is set.  Each category also has its own
    <cat>/runtests (same options, except -a and categories) and a binary
    stress-<cat> that takes
    -n procs, -i iters, -t secs, -T secs, -s seed, -d dir, -m size, -v, -x,
    -l, -L, -N and test names.  -l lists the tests the binary runs by default
    (named_only ones are left out); -N drops the leak report (and overrides -L).
    The net storm test (64 connects against a backlog of 5) allows 90 s for a
    pending connect: SYNs dropped on a full backlog are retransmitted in
    backed-off waves that each admit only about backlog connects; a connect
    may end in ECONNREFUSED, ETIMEDOUT or ECONNRESET.  The net
    flood test runs only when named and STRESS_EXHAUST=1: it exhausts files,
    sockets and pcbs with a bounded buffer fill.  STRESS_EXHAUST=2 fills
    every pair until EWOULDBLOCK, which is expected to wedge current kernels
    (a non-blocking sosend sleeps waiting for mbufs); never run it on a
    machine you need (GitHub #51).
    The pty test (proc) needs working ptys; mmap anon and anonshared are UNSUP
    where MAP_ANON is refused; mmap zero is
    UNSUP when /dev/zero is not a character device or mmap of it fails with
    EOPNOTSUPP, ENODEV or EINVAL, and FAILs when the map works but reads
    wrong data or any other call fails; the vm wire test falls back to syscalls
    177/178 where Libc lacks mlock/munlock; it squeezes physical memory, so
    vm/runtests leaves it out of the all-at-once phase (with pressure it
    thrashes a 128 MB guest past the watchdog).  The fs rename and link tests
    churn names in a tree shared by the workers; fs link is UNSUP where the
    file system has no hard links or symlinks; on HFS, rmdir or rename of an
    object in use fails with EBUSY, which ends an fs test as UNSUP
    (reason="EBUSY: in-use object") rather than FAIL; on NFS a call on a
    file or directory another worker removed after this worker looked it up
    fails with ESTALE (the client has no vnode locks, and a file handle cannot
    name a removed object), which ends the test as UNSUP
    (reason="ESTALE: object removed by another worker"); misc eintr has signals
    interrupt blocking calls, and FAILs (reason=no-eintr) if read, waitpid or
    flock never returned EINTR without SA_RESTART; like misc flock it is
    UNSUP where flock fails with EOPNOTSUPP (NFS, msdosfs).  misc fifo is
    UNSUP where mkfifo fails with EOPNOTSUPP, ENOSYS or EINVAL (msdosfs);
    net unix is UNSUP where the file system has no socket nodes (msdosfs:
    reason="connect: ENOTSOCK").
    mmap sync falls back to syscall 65 where Libc lacks msync.  mmap regrow
    shrinks a file while it is mapped, unmaps it and grows it back; read()
    must then return the pattern below the cut and zeros above.  It FAILs
    on kernels without the GitHub #59 fix.  A test whose workers vanish
    without being reaped (waitpid says there are no children while the
    leader still counts live ones) ends as FAIL reason=lost-child.
    vm pressure holds a working set of 1.5x RAM (or -m) and checks every
    page of it.  Its watchdog scales with that set: -t (or the 600 s
    default) times one per started 192 MB, the set of a 128 MB guest, which
    pages it in about 140-210 s on QEMU TCG.  A 512 MB guest's 768 MB takes
    about 820 s and gets 4x, 2400 s; a timeout reports the scaled secs.
    There is no cap: a 32-bit working set keeps the factor at most 22.
    -T secs ends a test normally (PASS) once secs have passed; -t is the watchdog.
    -T does not bound ipc msg/ool or net select: their two processes run a
    shared count, so stopping one end early would fail the other.

Other file systems (ffsbench stress --fs)
    vm/ffsbench.py stress --fs LIST runs the categories (default fs mmap) on
    other file systems as well as, or instead of, UFS: ext2, hfs, msdos, nfs,
    or all.  The workload disk is replaced by one targets disk with three
    128 MB partitions: a is an ext2 volume made on the host (WSL mke2fs; the
    image has no e2fsprogs), b is formatted HFS+ and c FAT16 on the guest.
    nfs is a loopback export of /mnt1/nfsexp (on the UFS scratch disk) made
    through NetInfo's /exports and mounted from localhost.  The guest builds
    mount_ext2fs, mount_hfs, newfs_hfs, newfs_msdos, mount_msdos and
    fsck_msdos from src/ext2fs-1, src/hfs-1 and src/msdosfs-1
    (vm/stress_fs.sh), runs `run` on each mount, unmounts and checks it:
    fsck_msdos on the guest, e2fsck and the tools/hfsimg checker on the host
    from the disk image.  result.txt gets a "targets" section per file system
    (ran, unsup with the reason, or lost when the guest stopped answering),
    and each target's results are fetched as soon as it finishes, so a later
    panic does not lose them.

Result lines
    STRESS <cat> <test> PASS iters=N procs=N ... ops=N maxlat=... seed=N
    STRESS <cat> <test> FAIL key=value ... seed=N
    STRESS <cat> <test> UNSUP reason="call: ERRNAME"

    PASS: the test finished.  FAIL: data was wrong, a call failed, a worker
    died (signal=N, exit=N) or the time limit hit (reason=timeout).  A FAIL
    a test reports itself carries worker=N iter=N.  The first failure stops
    the test's other workers and is the one reported.  UNSUP:
    the kernel lacks a feature the test needs; it is not a failure.  PASS lines
    may carry extra key=value fields.

Leak report
    Each result line may end with fields comparing the kernel before and after
    the test:  leak=<zone>:+N,...  wired=+N  mbufs=+N.  They appear only for
    growth beyond a threshold: a zone above +64 elements and +10%, wired pages
    above +32, mbufs above +64 (at most the 3 largest zones are named).  A
    test is not failed for it unless -L or STRESS_LEAK=fail is given, which
    turns the PASS into FAIL reason=leak.  Caches such as TCP TIME_WAIT can
    show growth on a healthy kernel.  The report is only meaningful for a
    test run alone: concurrent tests' kernel allocations land in each other's
    snapshots (pv_entry, map entries, objects, wired).  So every runtests
    passes -N in its all-at-once phase, and soak passes -N unless given -L.
    run lists such lines under "leaks:" in
    Report/summary.

Report/summary
    <cat> PASS=n FAIL=n UNSUP=n      one line per category
    STRESS ... FAIL ...              then every FAIL line

soak - a timed random mix
    soak [-t total] [-j slots] [-T test-secs] [-s seed] [-d dir] [-L] [cat ...]

    Runs randomly picked tests (as listed by stress-<cat> -l) of the categories
    (default: run's) for total seconds (default 3600), slots at a time (default 4),
    each for at most test-secs (default 60, the stress -T budget).  -t bounds
    when soak starts new jobs: a running job can overrun by up to its
    watchdog.  The seed (printed on the first
    line of Report/soak.out) picks the same tests again; timing still varies.
    soak runs its tests with -N; -L turns the leak report back on and fails
    tests that leak, but concurrent tests pollute each other's snapshots (see
    Leak report), so expect false leaks.  Output goes to Report/soak.out.
    Report/soak.summary is laid out like Report/summary.  Exit status is 1 if
    anything failed, 2 for bad usage or no tests found.  ffsbench stress --soak
    SECS runs it in a guest.
